  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The exact global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}-x$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 2$ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}} & =1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x+4} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+4 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-\\frac{15}{4}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,2],$ the critical points are the endpoints $x=-1,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\sqrt{-1+4}-(-1)=\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\ f(2) & =\\sqrt{2+4}-2=\\sqrt{6}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\sqrt{3}+1,$ so the global maximum is $\\sqrt{3}+1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(2)=\\sqrt{6}-2,$ so the global minimum is $\\sqrt{6}-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/ade599dd27bc3d018a3604620f3741ae05f8ebcc8ff583994da155ce7f311146.png)",
